 ('ii',r'\(\int\tan\theta\,d\theta\) =',[r'\(\ln|\sin\theta|+C\)',r'\(\ln|\cos\theta|+C\)',r'\(-\ln|\cos\theta|+C\)',r'\(-\ln|\sin\theta|+C\)'],'c',['Write tan θ = sin θ/cos θ.','Set u = cos θ, du = −sin θ dθ.','The integral becomes −∫du/u.','Thus the answer is −ln|cos θ|+C, option (c).','It holds on each interval where cos θ ≠ 0, using radians.']),
 ('iii',r'\(\int dx/\sqrt{a^2-x^2}\) =',[r'\(\ln|x+\sqrt{x^2+a^2}|+C\)',r'\(\sin^{-1}(a/x)+C\)',r'\(\sin^{-1}(x/a)+C\)',r'\(\cos^{-1}(x/a)+C\)'],'c, assuming a > 0',['For a > 0 and |x| < a, put u = x/a.','Then dx = a du and √(a²−x²) = a√(1−u²).','The integral becomes ∫du/√(1−u²) = arcsin u+C.','Return to x to obtain arcsin(x/a)+C, option (c) under the usual a > 0 convention.','For arbitrary nonzero a, use arcsin(x/|a|)+C; if a < 0 the printed (c) instead differentiates with the wrong sign.']),
 ('iv',r'\(\int\frac1{\sqrt{t^2-36}}\,dx\) =',[r'\(\frac1{2a}\ln|\frac{t-6}{t+6}|+C\)',r'\(\frac12\ln|\frac{t-6}{t+6}|+C\)',r'\(-\frac12(\ln|t/6+1|-\ln|t/6-1|+C)\)',r'\(\frac16(\ln|t/6+1|+6)+C\)'],'No correct printed option',['The source integrand uses t but the differential is dx; this mismatch must be stated rather than silently repaired.','If t is independent of x and |t| > 6, the literal x-antiderivative is x/√(t²−36)+C.','If the intended differential was dt, a primitive is ln|t+√(t²−36)|+C on each of t > 6 and t < −6.','Differentiate the logarithm: its derivative is 1/√(t²−36) on those open intervals.','The printed logarithms of (t−6)/(t+6) differentiate to rational terms proportional to 1/(t²−36), not the square-root integrand.','Neither the literal nor the intended square-root answer appears among the choices. The unrelated a in option (a) does not fix the mismatch.']),
 ('v',r'\(\int(x^3-4)\,dx\) =',[r'\(x^4/4-4x+C\)',r'\(x^3/3-4x+C\)',r'\(-x^4/4+4x+C\)',r'\(x^4/4+4x^3/3-4x^2/2-4x+C\)'],'a',['Apply the power rule to x³: its integral is x⁴/4.','Integrate the constant −4 to −4x.','Add the arbitrary constant C.','The result x⁴/4−4x+C matches (a).','Differentiate to recover x³−4.']),
 ('vi',r'\(\int f(x)g′(x)\,dx\) =',[r'\(f′(x)g(x)+\int g′(x)f(x)dx\)',r'\(f(x)g(x)-\int g(x)f′(x)dx\)',r'\(f′(x)g(x)-\int g′(x)f′(x)dx\)',r'\(f(x)g(x)-\int g(x)f(x)dx\)'],'b',['The product rule is (fg)′ = f′g+fg′.','Rearrange: fg′ = (fg)′−gf′.','Integrate both sides: ∫fg′dx = fg−∫gf′dx.','This is option (b), whose second integrand contains f′.','The visually checked option (d) contains f without the prime and is not the general parts rule.']),
 ('vii',r'\(\int\tan^4x\,dx\) =',[r'\((2/3)\tan^2x+x-\tan x+C\)',r'\((1/3)\tan^3x+x-\tan x+C\)',r'\((3/4)\tan^2x-x+\tan x+C\)',r'\(3\tan^3x+x+\tan x+C\)'],'b',['Use tan⁴x = tan²x(sec²x−1).','For ∫tan²x sec²x dx, set u = tan x and obtain tan³x/3.','The subtracted integral ∫tan²x dx = ∫(sec²x−1)dx = tan x−x.','Therefore the primitive is tan³x/3−tan x+x+C.','This matches (b), on intervals excluding cosine zeros.']),
 ('viii',r'\(\int\frac{x+8}{x^2-64}\,dx\) =',[r'\(\ln|x-8|+C\)',r'\((1/2)\ln|x^2-64|+(1/2)|x/8+1|-(1/2)\ln|x/2-1|+C\)',r'\((1/2)\ln|x^2-64|-(1/2)\ln|x/8+1|+(1/2)\ln|x/8-1|+C\)',r'\((1/2)\ln|x/8+1|+(1/2)\ln|x/8-1|+C\)'],'a and c',['Factor x²−64 = (x−8)(x+8). The original function excludes x = ±8.','On that domain the fraction simplifies to 1/(x−8).','A primitive is ln|x−8|+C, option (a).','In (c), expand ln|x²−64| as ln|x−8|+ln|x+8|, and expand the scaled arguments using ln|x/8±1| = ln|x±8|−ln 8.','The x+8 terms and scaling constants cancel; (c) also equals ln|x−8|+C.','Thus both (a) and (c) are correct on the original domain. The malformed (b) lacks ln in its middle term and uses x/2−1 in its last term.']),
